{"event_checks": [{"event": "An angel sends Philip toward the south", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 8:26 says the angel of the Lord told Philip to go toward the south."}, {"event": "Philip meets a man of Ethiopia", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 8:27 has Philip arise and go, and 'behold, a man of Ethiopia' appears. The actual approach comes in 8:29-30, but this is acceptable paraphrase."}, {"event": "Philip asks, Understandest thou what thou readest?", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 8:30 quotes this question word for word."}, {"event": "Philip baptizes the eunuch", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 8:38 says 'he baptized him.'"}, {"event": "The Spirit of the Lord catches Philip away", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 8:39 says 'the Spirit of the Lord caught away Philip.'"}], "order_correct": true, "order_ambiguous": "", "title_and_hint_ok": true, "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "seq-philip_ethiopian"}
{"event_checks": [{"event": "Cornelius sees an angel in a vision", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:3 states he saw in a vision an angel of God coming in to him."}, {"event": "Peter sees a great sheet let down from heaven", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:11 describes a vessel 'as it had been a great sheet' let down to the earth. The KJV calls it a vessel likened to a sheet, so the paraphrase is fine."}, {"event": "Cornelius waits with his kinsmen and friends", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:24 says Cornelius waited for them and had called together his kinsmen and near friends."}, {"event": "The Holy Ghost falls on all who hear", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:44 says the Holy Ghost fell on all them which heard the word."}, {"event": "Peter commands them to be baptized", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:48 says he commanded them to be baptized in the name of the Lord."}], "order_correct": true, "order_ambiguous": "", "title_and_hint_ok": true, "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "seq-peter_cornelius"}
{"event_checks": [{"event": "Herod kills James with the sword", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 12:2 states he killed James the brother of John with the sword. The 'he' is Herod the king, named in 12:1."}, {"event": "Peter sleeps between two soldiers", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 12:6 says Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains."}, {"event": "The chains fall off Peter's hands", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 12:7 says his chains fell off from his hands."}, {"event": "The iron gate opens of its own accord", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 12:10 says the iron gate opened to them of his own accord."}, {"event": "Rhoda is too glad to open the gate", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 12:14 says she opened not the gate for gladness. Rhoda is named in 12:13."}], "order_correct": true, "order_ambiguous": "", "title_and_hint_ok": true, "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "seq-peter_prison"}
{"event_checks": [{"event": "The Lord opens Lydia's heart", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:14 states 'whose heart the Lord opened.'"}, {"event": "Paul casts the spirit out of a damsel", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:18 has Paul commanding the spirit to come out of her. 'Damsel' comes from v16, but the event is clearly supported."}, {"event": "Paul and Silas are cast into prison", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:23: 'they cast them into prison.'"}, {"event": "At midnight they pray and sing praises", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:25 matches."}, {"event": "The jailer and all his household are baptized", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:33: 'was baptized, he and all his, straightway.' 'Household' is a fair paraphrase of 'all his' (cf. v31, 'thy house')."}], "order_correct": true, "order_ambiguous": "", "title_and_hint_ok": true, "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "seq-paul_philippi"}
{"event_checks": [{"event": "Hezekiah breaks the brasen serpent", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 18:4 explicitly says he 'brake in pieces the brasen serpent that Moses had made.'"}, {"event": "Hezekiah spreads the letter before the LORD", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 19:14 says he 'went up into the house of the LORD, and spread it before the LORD.'"}, {"event": "The angel of the LORD strikes the Assyrian camp", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 19:35 says the angel 'smote in the camp of the Assyrians an hundred fourscore and five thousand.'"}, {"event": "Hezekiah is sick unto death", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 20:1 says 'In those days was Hezekiah sick unto death.'"}, {"event": "The shadow goes back ten degrees", "supported_by_text": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 20:11 says the LORD 'brought the shadow ten degrees backward.'"}], "order_correct": false, "order_ambiguous": "The stated order matches the narrative order of 2 Kings 18–20, but not clearly the chronological order. 2 Kings 20:1 dates the illness only as 'In those days.' In 20:6, God promises, 'I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of Assyria,' which is future tense and implies the Assyrian threat was still pending. Also, the fifteen years added (20:6) plus Hezekiah's 29-year reign (18:2) place the illness in his 14th year, the same year as Sennacherib's invasion (18:13). An informed player could therefore reasonably place the sickness, and the shadow sign, before the angel's strike on the Assyrian camp, and possibly before the letter as well. Because the title just says 'Put these events in order,' both orderings are defensible.", "title_and_hint_ok": false, "verdict": "fix", "suggested_fix": "Option 1: retitle the item 'Put these events in the order 2 Kings records them: King Hezekiah' and change the hint to 'Follow the order of the narrative in 2 Kings 18–20; the first event is in 2 Kings 18.' Option 2: remove the chronological overlap. Keep the illness and shadow events, but replace the two Assyrian-deliverance events with events that clearly come before the illness, such as Hezekiah's reformations (18:4) and his rebellion against the king of Assyria (18:7). Alternatively, keep the Assyrian events and drop the 2 Kings 20 events, using something like Sennacherib's death in 19:37 instead.", "id": "seq-hezekiah"}
